# The Missing Link: Why PHP Auto-Complete Fails in Brackets

It is a common frustration when switching between languages in an editor. In Adobe Brackets, HTML and CSS auto-completion often works out of the box or with minimal setup. You type `div` or `margin`, press `Ctrl` + `Space`, and there it is. However, PHP behaves differently because the language is dynamic and context-dependent. The fact that your installed extensionsâIntegrated Development, PHP Syntax Hint, and QuickDocsPHPâare present does not guarantee that the underlying engine knows which functions are available to you at any specific moment.

The core issue preventing auto-completion for `mysql_` or modern Laravel helpers like `DB::table()` is that Brackets relies on static analysis. Unlike a compiler that reads all your code before building, an IDE needs to understand the current scope to suggest methods. Without explicit definitions of what functions exist in your file or project, the suggestion engine remains blind to PHP's runtime behavior.

To enable this functionality, you cannot simply rely on generic syntax highlighting extensions. You need to provide Brackets with a map of available functions and classes. This is typically done through configuration files that define the "scope." For legacy code involving `mysql_` functions, you would ideally include a specific definition file or ensure the extension knows the standard PHP function set.

In many modern setups, this involves configuring the `brackets-integrated-development` extension to recognize your project as a PHP application. You may need to create a `.brackets-config.json` file in your project root. Inside this file, you can specify the language settings that tell Brackets to treat the file as PHP and load the necessary definition files for auto-completion:

```json
{
    "language": [
        {
            "name": "PHP",
            "extensions": [".php"]
        }
    ],
    "completion": {
        "minPrefix": 3,
        "maxItems": 20
    }
}
```

This configuration ensures that when you start typing `mysql`, Brackets attempts to match it against known PHP function signatures. However, there is a significant catch with older versions of Brackets, such as the 0.41 mentioned in your setup. The built-in completion engine was notoriously weak for server-side languages compared to frontend ones like HTML and CSS.

The extensions you listed address syntax highlighting and quick documentation, but they do not necessarily inject the full list of global PHP functions into the completion provider unless explicitly told to do so. If you are working on a Laravel project, for instance, generic PHP definitions will fail to suggest framework-specific helpers. You would need a specialized extension that understands the Composer autoloader or the specific service container patterns used in your framework.

The most robust solution often involves switching to an extension dedicated to PHP code completion that actively scans your `vendor` directory or parses your class files to build a local index. Without this, pressing `Ctrl` + `Space` will only offer generic guesses based on the string you typed so far, ignoring the actual methods available on the objects you are working with.

If you continue to struggle with specific legacy functions like `mysql_connect`, remember that these were deprecated years ago in favor of PDO or MySQLi. While Brackets can be configured to recognize them if you provide a custom definition file, relying on active development and modern standards is usually the safer path forward. The editor must have a concrete list of available identifiers to suggest them; without that list, no amount of extension installation will trigger the completion popup for server-side logic.
